I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the reusable analysis scripts first. Then I’ll build a chemically balanced candidate inventory, health-screen every structural model, and refine/test the surviving phases in GSAS-II before freezing and replay-validating the deliverables.

The chemistry is a BaO–WO₃ reaction at 750 °C, so the first inventory must include BaWO₄, residual WO₃/BaCO₃, and charge-balanced non-1:1 barium tungstates if residual peak families demand them. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking what GSAS-II scripting support is available so candidate testing uses the measured profile directly.

The exact COD BaWO₄ record is chemically plausible but flagged for split half-occupied oxygen sites only 1.17 Å apart—a classic average/disorder representation, not a defensible final model without comparison. I’m therefore comparing it against ordered scheelite analogues (with Ba substituted on the divalent site) and multiple passing monoclinic WO₃ records under the same profile assumptions. One older WO₃ record already fails outright because it contains full-occupancy O–O contacts of 0.46 Å.

The peak-family check is internally consistent: the three WO₃ low-angle lines predicted at 23.12°, 23.58°, and 24.38° occur at 23.40°, 23.87°, and 24.66°, and their higher-angle split families recur near 33–34°, 42°, and 50–56°. This supports a two-phase starting model (scheelite BaWO₄ + γ-WO₃), while BaCO₃ and Ba-rich tungstates lack several required companion lines. I’m moving from identification to quantitative refinement, with all default size/strain terms explicitly neutralized before any profile decisions.

Adding Ba₃W₂O₉ resolves the previously coherent “unmodeled” families at 16.5/17.8°, 26.3°, 27.8°, 30.8°, 37.7°, 40.8°, 51.8°, 63.1–63.8°, 70.1°, 71.5°, and 88.6°. The three-phase fit drops Rwp from 9.30% to 6.62% and yields provisional crystalline fractions near 71.8% BaWO₄, 11.9% WO₃, and 16.3% Ba₃W₂O₉. I’m now replay-comparing the disordered exact BaWO₄ record against two passing ordered analogues; the fractions are not final yet.

The model replay is still running because each candidate is being reconverged through the full background/scale/cell/shift/profile sequence, not judged from a single substitution. Early converged files already show that the BaWO₄ oxygen model materially changes Rwp and the inferred minor-phase fractions, so this comparison is necessary before choosing the final structure.

The passing transformed-cell γ-WO₃ model is currently preferred: Rwp 6.068% versus 6.115% for the reviewed conventional record, whose 1.57 Å O–O contact has no compensating fit advantage. Two competing WO₃ polymorphs are still being replayed; after that I’ll test the residual/profile causes required by the gate rather than stopping at the current GOF ≈2.01.

The nuisance-model replays are taking longer than a single cycle because each branch is being reconverged four times. They cover exactly the unresolved alternatives visible in the residuals: polarization, background order, surface roughness, and zero offset versus specimen displacement. I’ll retain a term only if it improves the replay and remains physical/non-bound.

Two clear findings emerged: increasing background order barely helps (Rwp 6.068→6.046%), while roughness gives a large, physical improvement (Rwp 4.252%, GOF 1.41). Free polarization improves further but runs to 1.038, outside its physical 0–1 range, so I will not accept that unbounded result. I’m testing whether realistic fixed polarization and roughness are complementary or merely trading the same angular intensity trend.

The strongest remaining residual family is now identifiable rather than a profile artifact: 24.18°, 28.05°, 34.31/34.86°, 42.26°, 45.20°, and 47.26° are the shifted companion lines of orthorhombic BaCO₃. That is chemically expected unreacted precursor and its CIF passed the health screen. I’m adding it as a fourth phase and reconverging the complete model before deciding whether any phase-specific broadening is still warranted.

The four-phase replay is still converging; adding BaCO₃ introduces its own cell and scale while the existing roughness/profile terms readjust. This is expected to change all fractions, so I’m waiting for full convergence rather than quoting the first cycle.

The BaCO₃ addition is decisive: Rwp falls to 3.494% (Rexp 3.017%, GOF 1.158; Durbin–Watson 1.50), with provisional fractions 68.86% BaWO₄, 11.02% WO₃, 14.23% Ba₃W₂O₉, and 5.89% BaCO₃. A second passing BaCO₃ record, transformed to a compatible setting and replayed, fits worse (Rwp 3.684%), so the modern 25 °C witherite model is preferred. I’m now checking phase-specific widths and the weak-phase watch list against this four-phase baseline.

The quantitative width check shows why a targeted profile test is justified: strong BaWO₄ families are matched closely at 26.70° and 43.19°, but the Ba₃W₂O₉ family near 63.82° is broader in the observation (FWHM 0.243°) than in the calculation (0.167°). I’m comparing one common U term against Ba₃W₂O₉-only size and strain broadening from the same saved baseline.

These branches are computationally heavier because phase broadening derivatives are being refined alongside four cells, four scales, roughness, shift, background, and the common profile. The runs remain active; no additional terms will be accepted until the replayed Rwp and parameter values are stable.

The controlled broadening test favors Ba₃W₂O₉ microstrain: 1404 ppm gives Rwp 3.358% versus 3.423% for a 0.346 µm size model and 3.460% for common-U alone. I’m checking the combination because the strong BaWO₄ high-angle peaks also show a small common width deficit; this determines whether the effects are complementary.

The accepted profile now has a positive common U=4.17, W=17.73, X=2.94 and Ba₃W₂O₉ strain ≈1397 ppm; together they reduce Rwp to 3.339% (GOF 1.107) and are complementary. The watch-list replay is testing a passing small-cell WO₃ polymorph as a replacement and the reviewed Ba₃WO₆ record as a possible weak addition.

The four-phase model is converged and chemically balanced. I’m now doing the required frozen full-range replay, exporting directly from the reopened project, and cross-checking every deliverable against that saved state.

The reopened project replayed stably: Rwp 3.3385%, GOF about 1.107, with phase fractions changing by less than 0.001 wt% between replays. One roughness/scale correlation remains high, so I’ll report that as a systematic uncertainty rather than overstate the formal errors.

All four final exported CIFs pass the required occupancy/contact health check. I’m repeating the numerical line-shape spot checks on the final profile now, including the broadened Ba₃W₂O₉ family that drove the accepted strain term.

A focused residual scan found a low-angle split family near the WO₃ triplet that deserves one more inventory challenge. I’m testing whether it is genuine coexistence of a second WO₃ polymorph or simply unresolved/model-dependent splitting before I freeze the interpretation.

The residual family is explained by phase-specific WO₃ broadening, not a second phase: releasing one WO₃ width term lowers Rwp from 3.3385% to 3.1740% and raises Durbin–Watson from 1.64 to 1.81. Size and strain models are nearly tied; the slightly better, simpler size model gives an effective coherence length of 0.143(5) µm, so that microstructural interpretation will be marked as model-dependent.

The final replay now passes all consistency checks: the four fractions sum exactly to 1, all 8,301 profile rows reproduce the reopened project arrays, every CIF path exists, and CIF cells/formulas agree with the project. I’m writing the concise evidence and uncertainty record next.

Completed and cross-validated against the reopened GSAS-II project.

- BaWO₄: 65.829 wt%
- Ba₃W₂O₉: 15.695 wt%
- WO₃: 13.344 wt%
- BaCO₃: 5.132 wt%
- Rwp: 3.1740%; GOF: 1.0521

The final CIFs pass the structural health checks. Main uncertainties are a roughly 1–3 wt% model-dependent fraction uncertainty and unresolved size-versus-strain origin of WO₃ broadening.

